                 BROWSER DEVELOPER TOOLS
======================================================================

SOURCE:
A_Overview.txt — Phase 7: Web App Testing

CHAPTER GOAL
------------
Learn how a QA Engineer uses Browser Developer Tools (DevTools)
to inspect webpages, debug UI issues, analyze HTTP requests,
inspect responses, and validate browser-side data.

Topics covered:
23.1  Elements
23.2  Console
23.3  Network
23.4  Sources
23.5  Application
23.6  Storage
23.7  Cookies
23.8  Local Storage
23.9  Session Storage
23.10 HTTP Requests
23.11 Response Inspection


======================================================================
23.1 ELEMENTS
======================================================================

The Elements panel allows a tester to inspect the HTML structure
and CSS styling of a webpage.

Typical uses:

    • Inspect HTML elements
    • Inspect attributes
    • Inspect CSS
    • Check element visibility
    • Inspect buttons, forms, inputs, links, etc.
    • Find locators for automation
    • Temporarily modify HTML/CSS
    • Understand page structure


Example:

HTML:

    <button id="loginBtn" class="primary-btn">
        Login
    </button>

Using Elements, a tester can inspect:

    tag       = button
    id        = loginBtn
    class     = primary-btn
    text      = Login


Why is this important for QA?

Suppose the Login button is not visible.

The tester can inspect:

    • Is the element present?
    • Is display:none applied?
    • Is visibility:hidden applied?
    • Is the element outside the visible area?
    • Is another element covering it?
    • Is the CSS incorrect?


Example CSS:

    button {
        display: none;
    }

The button exists in HTML but is not visible.

This can help identify the cause of a UI defect.


--------------------------------------------------------------
IMPORTANT ELEMENTS CONCEPTS
--------------------------------------------------------------

HTML structure:

    html
      |
      +-- head
      |
      +-- body
            |
            +-- header
            |
            +-- main
            |
            +-- footer


A QA tester should understand:

    • parent element
    • child element
    • sibling element
    • attributes
    • classes
    • IDs
    • DOM structure


--------------------------------------------------------------
LOCATORS
--------------------------------------------------------------

Elements panel is extremely useful for automation testing.

Example:

    <input id="username"
           name="username"
           class="login-input"
           type="text">


Possible locators:

    ID:
        #username

    CSS:
        input.login-input

    Attribute:
        input[name="username"]

    XPath:
        //input[@id='username']


This becomes important when using:

    • Selenium
    • Playwright
    • Cypress
    • other automation tools


--------------------------------------------------------------
QA EXAMPLE
--------------------------------------------------------------

Requirement:

    "Login button should be enabled after valid credentials."

Tester enters valid credentials.

Login button remains disabled.

Using Elements:

    1. Inspect button.
    2. Check disabled attribute.
    3. Check CSS.
    4. Check DOM changes.
    5. Inspect related elements.
    6. Use Console/Network if required.

Possible finding:

    <button disabled>

This provides useful debugging information.


======================================================================
23.2 CONSOLE
======================================================================

The Console displays JavaScript messages, warnings, errors,
and other browser-side information.

Common console information:

    • Errors
    • Warnings
    • Logs
    • JavaScript output
    • Exceptions


Example:

    Uncaught TypeError:
    Cannot read properties of undefined


A QA tester can use this information to understand why
a webpage feature is not working.


--------------------------------------------------------------
COMMON CONSOLE LEVELS
--------------------------------------------------------------

    LOG
        General information.

    INFO
        Informational message.

    WARN
        Warning.

    ERROR
        Error.

    DEBUG
        Debugging information.


Example JavaScript:

    console.log("Login page loaded");

The message appears in Console.


--------------------------------------------------------------
QA USE CASE
--------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose:

    Requirement:
    Clicking "Submit" should display confirmation.

Actual behavior:

    Nothing happens.

Tester can:

    1. Open DevTools.
    2. Open Console.
    3. Click Submit.
    4. Observe errors.

Possible result:

    Uncaught ReferenceError:
    submitForm is not defined


This gives developers useful information.


--------------------------------------------------------------
CONSOLE FOR BASIC TESTING
--------------------------------------------------------------

A tester may also execute simple JavaScript commands
when debugging.

For example:

    document.title

This can return the current page title.

Another example:

    document.querySelector("#loginBtn")

This can locate an element.


IMPORTANT:

Console is primarily a debugging and inspection tool.
Changing data through the console should only be done
in an authorized testing environment.


======================================================================
23.3 NETWORK
======================================================================

The Network panel is one of the most important DevTools
features for a QA Engineer.

It allows the tester to inspect network activity generated
by the browser.


A network request generally contains:

    Request
       |
       +-- URL
       +-- Method
       +-- Headers
       +-- Parameters
       +-- Body
       |
       v
    Server
       |
       v
    Response
       |
       +-- Status
       +-- Headers
       +-- Body


--------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT CAN BE INSPECTED?
--------------------------------------------------------------

The Network panel can help inspect:

    • HTTP requests
    • HTTP responses
    • Request methods
    • Status codes
    • Request headers
    • Response headers
    • Query parameters
    • Request payload
    • Response body
    • Timing
    • API calls
    • Static resources


--------------------------------------------------------------
EXAMPLE
--------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose the tester clicks:

    Login

Browser sends:

    POST /api/login

Network panel may show:

    Request URL:
        https://example.com/api/login

    Method:
        POST

    Status:
        200

    Request Payload:
        {
            "username": "user1",
            "password": "*****"
        }

    Response:
        {
            "token": "...",
            "user": "user1"
        }


This allows the tester to determine whether the
frontend and backend are communicating correctly.


--------------------------------------------------------------
IMPORTANT NETWORK STATUS CODES
--------------------------------------------------------------

    200     OK
    201     Created
    204     No Content

    400     Bad Request
    401     Unauthorized
    403     Forbidden
    404     Not Found

    500     Internal Server Error
    502     Bad Gateway
    503     Service Unavailable


Example:

Expected:

    GET /products
    → 200

Actual:

    GET /products
    → 500


This can indicate a server-side problem.


======================================================================
23.4 SOURCES
======================================================================

The Sources panel allows inspection and debugging of
frontend source files.

Common resources include:

    • JavaScript files
    • CSS files
    • HTML-related resources
    • Source maps
    • Other loaded resources


It can be useful when investigating frontend behavior.


--------------------------------------------------------------
QA USE CASE
--------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose clicking a button produces unexpected behavior.

Tester can:

    1. Open Sources.
    2. Locate relevant JavaScript.
    3. Inspect code.
    4. Investigate execution.
    5. Work with developers to identify the problem.


For a QA Engineer, understanding the Sources panel is
particularly useful when performing advanced debugging.


======================================================================
23.5 APPLICATION
======================================================================

The Application panel allows inspection of browser-side
application data and storage.

It can provide access to information such as:

    • Cookies
    • Local Storage
    • Session Storage
    • Other browser-managed application data


This is especially useful when testing:

    • Login/session behavior
    • Authentication
    • User preferences
    • Browser-side state
    • Logout behavior


Example:

After login:

    Application
        |
        +-- Cookies
        |
        +-- Local Storage
        |
        +-- Session Storage


A tester can inspect whether expected data is stored.


======================================================================
23.6 STORAGE
======================================================================

Web applications often store information in the browser.

Important storage mechanisms include:

    1. Cookies
    2. Local Storage
    3. Session Storage


These mechanisms are different and should not be treated
as interchangeable.


--------------------------------------------------------------
HIGH-LEVEL COMPARISON
--------------------------------------------------------------

    Storage Type       Lifetime / Behavior

    Cookies
    ------------------------------
    Often associated with requests
    and can have expiration rules.

    Local Storage
    ------------------------------
    Persists in the browser until
    explicitly cleared.

    Session Storage
    ------------------------------
    Associated with the browser
    session/tab and is temporary.


Exact behavior depends on implementation and browser rules.


======================================================================
23.7 COOKIES
======================================================================

Cookies are small pieces of data associated with a website.

They can be used for:

    • Session management
    • Authentication-related state
    • Preferences
    • Tracking
    • Other application purposes


Example:

    sessionId = ABC123


A browser may send a cookie with an HTTP request.


--------------------------------------------------------------
COOKIE ATTRIBUTES
--------------------------------------------------------------

Important cookie attributes include:

    Name
    Value
    Domain
    Path
    Expiration
    Secure
    HttpOnly
    SameSite


--------------------------------------------------------------
SECURE
--------------------------------------------------------------

A Secure cookie is intended to be sent over secure
connections such as HTTPS.


--------------------------------------------------------------
HTTPONLY
--------------------------------------------------------------

An HttpOnly cookie is not directly accessible to
JavaScript through the normal document.cookie interface.

This is relevant to security testing.


--------------------------------------------------------------
SAMESITE
--------------------------------------------------------------

SameSite controls aspects of when cookies are sent
in cross-site contexts.

It is relevant to:

    • authentication
    • session behavior
    • cross-site request scenarios


--------------------------------------------------------------
QA TESTING EXAMPLE
--------------------------------------------------------------

Requirement:

    User should remain logged in during a valid session.

Tester:

    1. Login.
    2. Open Application.
    3. Inspect cookies.
    4. Check relevant session/authentication state.
    5. Perform navigation.
    6. Verify session behavior.


Possible defect:

    User unexpectedly becomes logged out
    after navigating to another page.


The tester can inspect browser storage and network
requests to investigate.


======================================================================
23.8 LOCAL STORAGE
======================================================================

Local Storage provides browser-side key/value storage.

Example:

    theme = dark

    language = en

    userPreference = compact


Data stored in Local Storage generally persists
across browser sessions until it is cleared.


--------------------------------------------------------------
QA USE CASES
--------------------------------------------------------------

Local Storage can be useful for testing:

    • Preferences
    • Client-side application state
    • UI configuration
    • Stored identifiers
    • Feature-related browser state


Example:

Requirement:

    "Selected language should remain English
     after reopening the browser."

Tester:

    1. Select English.
    2. Inspect Local Storage.
    3. Close/reopen browser.
    4. Reopen application.
    5. Verify language.


If the application incorrectly loses the preference,
the tester can investigate Local Storage behavior.


======================================================================
23.9 SESSION STORAGE
======================================================================

Session Storage provides temporary browser-side storage
associated with the current browsing session/context.


Example:

    currentStep = 3

    temporaryFilter = "electronics"


It can be used by applications for temporary state.


--------------------------------------------------------------
QA EXAMPLE
--------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose a multi-step form stores:

    currentStep = 2

The tester can:

    1. Navigate through the form.
    2. Inspect Session Storage.
    3. Refresh the page.
    4. Navigate between pages.
    5. Verify expected state.
    6. Test session/tab behavior.


A defect might occur if temporary data is unexpectedly
lost or incorrectly retained.


======================================================================
23.10 HTTP REQUESTS
======================================================================

An HTTP request is a message sent by a client to a server.

Typical structure:

    HTTP Request
    -------------------------
    Method
    URL
    Headers
    Parameters
    Body
    -------------------------


Example:

    POST /api/login HTTP/1.1

    Host: example.com
    Content-Type: application/json

    {
        "username": "user1",
        "password": "*****"
    }


--------------------------------------------------------------
HTTP METHODS
--------------------------------------------------------------

    GET
        Retrieve data.

    POST
        Send/create data.

    PUT
        Replace/update a resource.

    PATCH
        Partially update a resource.

    DELETE
        Delete a resource.


--------------------------------------------------------------
QUERY PARAMETERS
--------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    GET /products?category=mobile


Here:

    category = mobile

is a query parameter.


--------------------------------------------------------------
PATH PARAMETER
--------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    GET /products/101


Here:

    101

may identify a specific product.


--------------------------------------------------------------
REQUEST HEADERS
--------------------------------------------------------------

Examples:

    Content-Type
    Authorization
    Accept
    Cookie


Headers provide metadata associated with the request.


--------------------------------------------------------------
REQUEST BODY
--------------------------------------------------------------

Commonly used with methods such as:

    POST
    PUT
    PATCH


Example JSON:

    {
        "name": "Laptop",
        "price": 50000
    }


======================================================================
23.11 RESPONSE INSPECTION
======================================================================

After the server processes a request, it sends a response.

Typical response information:

    Status Code
    Headers
    Body


Example:

    HTTP/1.1 200 OK

    Content-Type: application/json


    {
        "id": 101,
        "name": "Laptop",
        "price": 50000
    }


A QA Engineer should inspect whether the response
matches the expected behavior.


--------------------------------------------------------------
RESPONSE VALIDATION
--------------------------------------------------------------

Check:

    1. Status code
    2. Response headers
    3. Response body
    4. Data values
    5. Error messages
    6. Response behavior
    7. Timing when relevant


--------------------------------------------------------------
EXAMPLE
--------------------------------------------------------------

Requirement:

    Valid product ID should return product information.


Request:

    GET /api/products/101


Expected:

    Status = 200

    Response:
    {
        "id": 101,
        "name": "Laptop"
    }


Actual:

    Status = 404

This may represent a defect depending on the
requirements and test conditions.


======================================================================
COMPLETE DEVTOOLS QA WORKFLOW
======================================================================

Suppose:

    User clicks "Place Order"
    but the order is not created.


A QA Engineer can investigate using DevTools.


STEP 1 — ELEMENTS
-----------------

Check:

    • Is the button present?
    • Is it enabled?
    • Is the correct element being clicked?
    • Are there UI/DOM issues?


STEP 2 — CONSOLE
----------------

Click the button and check:

    • JavaScript errors
    • Warnings
    • Exceptions


STEP 3 — NETWORK
----------------

Look for:

    POST /api/orders


Check:

    • Request URL
    • Method
    • Headers
    • Payload
    • Status code
    • Response


STEP 4 — RESPONSE
-----------------

Check:

    • Did server return expected response?
    • Is response data correct?
    • Is there an error message?


STEP 5 — APPLICATION
--------------------

Check:

    • Cookies
    • Local Storage
    • Session Storage


STEP 6 — SOURCES
----------------

If frontend behavior appears incorrect,
investigate the relevant source resources.


This workflow helps a QA Engineer move from:

    UI
      ↓
    Browser
      ↓
    JavaScript
      ↓
    HTTP Request
      ↓
    Backend
      ↓
    HTTP Response
      ↓
    Browser
      ↓
    UI


======================================================================
PRACTICAL SCENARIO 1 — LOGIN FAILURE
======================================================================

Problem:

    User enters valid username/password,
    clicks Login, but remains on Login page.


QA investigation:

    Elements
        ↓
    Verify Login button/form
        ↓
    Console
        ↓
    Check JavaScript errors
        ↓
    Network
        ↓
    Find login API
        ↓
    Inspect request
        ↓
    Inspect response
        ↓
    Application
        ↓
    Check authentication/session state


Possible findings:

    Case 1:
        Request never sent.

    Case 2:
        Request sent but server returns 401.

    Case 3:
        Server returns 200 but frontend
        fails to process response.

    Case 4:
        Authentication state is not stored correctly.


Each case can lead to a different defect or investigation path.


======================================================================
PRACTICAL SCENARIO 2 — PRODUCT PAGE DOES NOT LOAD
======================================================================

Requirement:

    Product details should load after selecting a product.


Tester:

    1. Open Elements.
    2. Verify product element.
    3. Open Network.
    4. Find product API request.
    5. Inspect status.
    6. Inspect response.
    7. Check Console for frontend errors.


Possible result:

    GET /api/products/101

    Status:
        500


Response:

    {
        "error": "Internal Server Error"
    }


This gives strong evidence that the API/server
returned an error.


======================================================================
PRACTICAL SCENARIO 3 — LOGOUT ISSUE
======================================================================

Requirement:

    After logout, the user should no longer be
    authenticated.


Tester:

    1. Login.
    2. Open Application.
    3. Inspect cookies/storage.
    4. Click Logout.
    5. Inspect storage again.
    6. Navigate to protected page.
    7. Inspect Network requests if necessary.


Expected:

    Authentication state should be removed/invalidated
    according to application behavior.


If protected content remains accessible unexpectedly,
this can be an important defect/security concern.


======================================================================
DEVTOOLS — IMPORTANT COMPARISON
======================================================================

    ELEMENTS
    --------
    Inspect webpage structure and CSS.

    CONSOLE
    -------
    Inspect JavaScript messages/errors.

    NETWORK
    -------
    Inspect HTTP requests/responses.

    SOURCES
    -------
    Inspect frontend source resources.

    APPLICATION
    -----------
    Inspect application/browser-managed data.

    COOKIES
    -------
    Inspect cookie-based browser data.

    LOCAL STORAGE
    -------------
    Inspect persistent browser-side storage.

    SESSION STORAGE
    ---------------
    Inspect temporary session/browser storage.


======================================================================
ELEMENTS vs CONSOLE vs NETWORK
======================================================================

    Question:
    "Where should I investigate?"


    UI/HTML/CSS problem
        → Elements


    JavaScript error
        → Console


    API/request/response problem
        → Network


    Browser-side storage problem
        → Application


    Frontend source/debugging problem
        → Sources


======================================================================
COMMON QA INTERVIEW QUESTIONS
======================================================================

Q1. What is Browser Developer Tools?

Answer:
Browser Developer Tools are browser-provided tools used
to inspect and debug web applications, including HTML,
CSS, JavaScript, network requests, responses, and
browser-side application data.


Q2. Why is DevTools important for a QA Engineer?

Answer:
It helps investigate UI problems, JavaScript errors,
HTTP requests/responses, API failures, cookies, storage,
and browser-side behavior.


Q3. What is the Elements panel used for?

Answer:
It is used to inspect the webpage's HTML/DOM structure,
attributes, and CSS.


Q4. What is the Console used for?

Answer:
It is used to view JavaScript logs, warnings, errors,
and other browser-side messages.


Q5. What is the Network panel used for?

Answer:
It is used to inspect HTTP/network activity including
requests, responses, headers, payloads, status codes,
and related timing information.


Q6. How do you investigate an API failure using DevTools?

Answer:

    1. Open Network.
    2. Perform the application action.
    3. Locate the API request.
    4. Check URL and method.
    5. Inspect request headers/body.
    6. Check status code.
    7. Inspect response.
    8. Compare with expected behavior.


Q7. What is Local Storage?

Answer:
It is browser-side key/value storage used by web
applications for data that generally persists until
cleared.


Q8. What is Session Storage?

Answer:
It is browser-side storage intended for temporary
session-related state.


Q9. What are cookies used for?

Answer:
Cookies can be used for session management,
authentication-related state, preferences, tracking,
and other application purposes.


Q10. What is the difference between Console and Network?

Answer:

    Console:
        JavaScript/browser messages and errors.

    Network:
        HTTP/network requests and responses.


Q11. What information can you inspect in a Network request?

Answer:

    • URL
    • Method
    • Headers
    • Parameters
    • Payload/body
    • Status
    • Response


Q12. A button works visually but no API request is sent.
How would you investigate?

Answer:

    1. Inspect the button using Elements.
    2. Check Console for JavaScript errors.
    3. Open Network.
    4. Click the button again.
    5. Verify whether a request is generated.
    6. If no request exists, investigate frontend behavior.


======================================================================
REAL QA BUG EXAMPLE
======================================================================

BUG TITLE:

    Login API returns 401 for valid credentials.


TEST STEPS:

    1. Open application.
    2. Enter valid username.
    3. Enter valid password.
    4. Click Login.
    5. Open DevTools → Network.
    6. Inspect login API request.


EXPECTED:

    Login API should authenticate the valid user
    and return the expected successful response.


ACTUAL:

    Login API returns:

        401 Unauthorized


EVIDENCE:

    • Network request
    • Request payload (without exposing sensitive secrets)
    • Status code
    • Response
    • Console error if applicable


This is much stronger evidence than simply reporting:

    "Login is not working."


======================================================================
IMPORTANT SECURITY PRACTICE
======================================================================

While using DevTools, testers may encounter:

    • passwords
    • tokens
    • session identifiers
    • personal information
    • API keys


Do NOT expose sensitive information unnecessarily.

When creating bug reports:

    • Mask passwords.
    • Avoid exposing secrets.
    • Mask sensitive tokens.
    • Follow organizational security policies.
    • Use only authorized environments.


======================================================================
QUICK REVISION
======================================================================

    DevTools
        ↓
    Browser debugging/inspection

    Elements
        ↓
    HTML + DOM + CSS

    Console
        ↓
