    Network
        ↓
    HTTP requests/responses

    Sources
        ↓
    Frontend source resources

    Application
        ↓
    Browser/application data

    Cookies
        ↓
    Cookie-based state

    Local Storage
        ↓
    Persistent browser-side storage

    Session Storage
        ↓
    Temporary session-related storage


Network investigation:

    Action
      ↓
    Request
      ↓
    Method
      ↓
    Headers
      ↓
    Payload
      ↓
    Status
      ↓
    Response
      ↓
    Validate expected behavior


======================================================================
JOB-READY CHECKLIST
======================================================================

Before moving forward, you should be able to:

[ ] Open Browser DevTools.

[ ] Inspect HTML using Elements.

[ ] Inspect CSS.

[ ] Identify useful element attributes.

[ ] Understand basic locators.

[ ] Open Console.

[ ] Identify JavaScript errors.

[ ] Open Network.

[ ] Find an API request.

[ ] Identify HTTP method.

[ ] Inspect request URL.

[ ] Inspect request headers.

[ ] Inspect request parameters.

[ ] Inspect request payload.

[ ] Identify HTTP status code.

[ ] Inspect response body.

[ ] Understand basic cookies.

[ ] Inspect cookies.

[ ] Inspect Local Storage.

[ ] Inspect Session Storage.

[ ] Understand the Application panel.

[ ] Use DevTools to investigate login failures.

[ ] Use DevTools to investigate API failures.

[ ] Use DevTools evidence while reporting bugs.

[ ] Avoid exposing sensitive credentials/tokens.


======================================================================
CHAPTER 23 — FINAL TAKEAWAY
======================================================================

A QA Engineer should not test a web application only
from the visible UI.

A modern web application involves:

    UI
     ↓
    Browser
     ↓
    JavaScript
     ↓
    HTTP Request
     ↓
    Backend/API
     ↓
    HTTP Response
     ↓
    Browser Storage/State
     ↓
    UI


Browser Developer Tools allow QA Engineers to observe
these layers and investigate where a problem occurs.

The most important panels for everyday QA work are:

    Elements
    Console
    Network
    Application

Among these, Network is especially important for
understanding API communication and diagnosing whether
a problem is occurring in the frontend, request,
backend response, or browser-side state.


